   "t": "Вообще-то у нас законный перерыв.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Я людям перерабатывать не дам.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sveta",
   "t": "Хорошо, я так и передам.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sveta",
   "t": "Кофе со сливками специально для вас.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Coffee with cream especially for you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'coffee' before landing on 'you'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Прошу внимания, вся наша команда!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Attention please, our entire team!' The meaning hinges on 'ask for' working together with 'team' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Как старший объявляю сейчас перерыв.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'As senior chef I declare a break now.' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'how' and 'older' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Кофе со сливками всем бесплатно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Coffee with cream for everyone for free.' Fedya anchors the line on 'coffee', while 'for free' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Ну, разумеется, кроме нашего Сени.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, of course, except for our Senya.' The grammar packs 'come on' and 'Senya' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Своих конкурентов я не угощаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I do not treat my competitors.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'one's own' and finishing on 'I treat'. Senya answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sveta",
   "t": "Вы коня моего не видели?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Have you seen my horse?' Senya brings out the contrast between 'you' and 'horse' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "А вы в шкафчике посмотрите.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Take a look inside the locker.' Notice how Russian puts 'locker / cabinet' and 'look' together in a short, direct phrase where word endings show how the ideas connect.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sveta",
   "t": "Нужны горячие медальоны для гостя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Hot medallions are needed for a guest.' Notice how Russian expresses 'needed' and 'guest' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Fedya answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Вообще-то у нас законный перерыв.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Actually, we are on a legal break.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'actually' before landing on 'break'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Я людям перерабатывать не дам.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I will not let my people overwork.' The meaning hinges on 'I' working together with 'will give' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sveta",
   "t": "Хорошо, я так и передам.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Fine, I will pass that along.' Sveta anchors the line on 'well', while 'will pass on' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
